<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Build.Locator" Version="1.11.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Workspaces" Version="5.6.0" />
    <!-- MSBuildWorkspace lives here, not in CSharp.Workspaces. Its MSBuild assemblies are
         resolved at runtime by MSBuildLocator - see SolutionLoader for the ordering rule. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Workspaces.MSBuild" Version="5.6.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Phase 3 reads EF Core's IModel to map entity->table and property->column. That requires the
    target's compiled DbContext types to be loaded into THIS process, which forces a decision
    about assembly identity. See docs/phase-3-notes.md for the full reasoning; the short version:

      - The EF/Npgsql assemblies below are UNIFIED with the target: TargetModelLoadContext returns
        null for these names so they resolve from FlowLens's own TPA. That is what makes
        typeof(DbContext) the same Type on both sides of the load-context boundary.
      - Versions are pinned to what the target actually ships (its Host deps.json says EF 10.0.9 /
        Npgsql provider 10.0.2). The TPA binds by simple name and IGNORES version, so a version
        below the target's would bind silently and fail later as MissingMethodException.
        EfVersionGate turns that into a loud pre-flight failure instead.
      - Because the unified set is exactly this package list, NuGet restore (NU1605) is the
        build-time enforcement mechanism. That is the structural difference from the MSBL001
        trap documented in Directory.Build.props: no runtime resolver hook is involved.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="10.0.9" />
    <PackageReference Include="Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL" Version="10.0.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The target is an ASP.NET Core app, so its assemblies reference the ASP.NET Core shared
    framework - Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions among others. Those live in the shared
    framework rather than in the target's output, so nothing on disk can supply them and the load
    context cannot invent them: without this, loading a module assembly raises
    ReflectionTypeLoadException for its hosted-service types.

    Measured, not guessed: three Infrastructure assemblies each reported exactly one unloadable
    type before this was added. No ASP.NET Core code is executed - this only widens the TPA so the
    target's own references resolve.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
